</test_inputs>
Do not provide any other text or markdown fences.

Here is the problem description:
<description>
Sometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. 
</description>

Here is the input specification:
<input_spec>
The first line contains the email address description. It is guaranteed that that is a proper email address with all the dots replaced by dot an the at signs replaced by at. The line is not empty and its length does not exceed 100 symbols.
</input_spec>


Here are sample inputs:
<sample_inputs>
<input>
vasyaatgmaildotcom
</input>
<input>
dotdotdotatdotdotat
</input>
<input>
aatt
</input>
</sample_inputs>

Here is the source code:
<source_code>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string str;
    cin >> str;

    string result;
    bool atFound = false;
    int i = 0;

    while (i < str.length()) {
        if (i + 2 < str.length() && str.substr(i, 3) == "dot") {
            // Check if adding dot would create invalid pattern (starting/ending with dot or consecutive dots)
            if (result.empty() || result.back() == '.') {
                // Skip this "dot" to avoid leading dot or consecutive dots
                i += 3;
                continue;
            }
            // Check if next character after "dot" would make next token invalid
            if (i + 3 < str.length() && str.substr(i + 3, 3) == "dot" && i + 3 == str.length() - 3) {
                // Avoid trailing dot
                i += 3;
                continue;
            }
            result += '.';
            i += 3;
        }
        else if (i + 1 < str.length() && str.substr(i, 2) == "at" && !atFound) {
            // Check if @ would be at start or end
            if (result.empty() || i + 2 == str.length()) {
                i += 2;
                continue;
            }
            result += '@';
            atFound = true;
            i += 2;
        }
        else {
            result += str[i];
            i++;
        }
    }

    // Handle case where we didn't process the last character properly
    // Also need to handle the case where "at" or "dot" at the very end
    if (!atFound) {
        // Try to find "at" that we might have missed
        for (int j = 0; j < (int)str.length() - 1; j++) {
            if (str.substr(j, 2) == "at" && j > 0 && j + 2 < str.length()) {
                // Reconstruct with this @
                string temp;
                for (int k = 0; k < j; k++) {
                    if (k + 2 < str.length() && str.substr(k, 3) == "dot") {
                        if (!temp.empty() && temp.back() != '.') {
                            temp += '.';
                            k += 2;
                        }
                    } else if (k + 1 < str.length() && str.substr(k, 2) == "at") {
                        if (k > 0 && k + 2 < str.length()) {
                            temp += '@';
                            k += 1;
                        }
                    } else {
                        temp += str[k];
                    }
                }
                // This approach is getting complex, let's use a cleaner method
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Actually, let's rewrite with a cleaner approach
    string finalResult;
    bool atUsed = false;
    int pos = 0;

    while (pos < str.length()) {
        if (!atUsed && pos + 1 < str.length() && str[pos] == 'a' && str[pos+1] == 't') {
            if (pos > 0 && pos + 2 < str.length()) {
                finalResult += '@';
                atUsed = true;
                pos += 2;
            } else {
                finalResult += str[pos];
                pos++;
            }
        }
        else if (pos + 2 < str.length() && str[pos] == 'd' && str[pos+1] == 'o' && str[pos+2] == 't') {
            if (!finalResult.empty() && finalResult.back() != '.') {
                if (pos + 3 < str.length()) {
                    finalResult += '.';
                    pos += 3;
                } else {
                    // Would create trailing dot, skip
                    finalResult += str[pos];
                    pos++;
                }
            } else {
                finalResult += str[pos];
                pos++;
            }
        }
        else {
            finalResult += str[pos];
            pos++;
        }
    }

    cout << finalResult << endl;
    return 0;
}
